## Re-run 2026-10-04: 130 checks, and the 28 newest ones were recorded in the wrong file

`scripts/remote-acceptance-in-docker.sh` grew from 102 checks to 130 in `6013a862`
(2026-10-03), which made `chaos-remote install` refuse an artifact the host was never told to
trust. That commit came with a full 130-check transcript -- in
`docs/verification/remote-provenance-linux-2026-10-02.log`, the file written about the
feature, not the file named after the lab. So the checks did run. The lab's own transcript
above still ended at `all 102 M4 acceptance checks passed`, 61 commits back at `f3a058a7`, and
nothing connected the two files: a reader of this document would conclude the lab has 102
checks, and the 130-check run that contradicts it is not findable from here. The lab was also
named by no workflow, so the gap could only be closed by someone remembering.

This run closes it at `36d0809e`, 15:46 +0800, and it is the first run of this lab recorded in
this file since the count changed. Two sections are quoted rather than all 239 lines: the
section the count grew by, and the one pre-existing section that gained a check. What the two
transcripts do and do not agree on is written out under them.

    $ bash scripts/remote-acceptance-in-docker.sh

    == an artifact is installed only if a key the host was told about signed these bytes
       ok  the publisher key is 32 raw ed25519 bytes, base64
       ok  the sidecar is the wrapped base64 a shell pipeline leaves behind
       ok  that host says at startup whose signature it will accept
       ok  an artifact with no signature is refused by a host with a key
       ok  the same build is installed once its sidecar is there
       ok  the sidecar was found next to the artifact, not named on the command line
       ok  a signature from a key this host was never told about is refused
       ok  bytes changed after signing are refused though their digest is correct
       ok  a file that is not a signature at all is refused by name
       ok  the four refusals published nothing
       ok  and left no half-written upload behind in the install directory
       ok  what it did install is byte-identical to what the key signed
       ok  the same unsigned build installs on the host that opted out
       ok  a keyless host says at startup that every install will be refused
       ok  a keyless host refuses an unsigned deploy
       ok  and refuses a correctly signed one too, because it has nothing to check it against
       ok  the keyless host published nothing at all
       ok  the environment switch is reported at startup as well
       ok  CHAOS_REMOTE_REQUIRE_SIGNATURE=0 installs the unsigned build
       ok  opting out of the requirement does not let a host accept a signature it cannot check
       ok  that host says both things at once: unsigned is accepted, a key is held
       ok  it installs the unsigned build, because that is what it opted into
       ok  and still refuses a signature its own key did not make
       ok  while the build that key did sign still installs
       ok  a build for another platform is refused by the host it was sent to
       ok  the platform refusal published nothing
       ok  and the host is still pointing at what it had

The 28th of those checks landed inside a section that already existed, and it is quoted here
as the run printed it:

    == starting the server, loopback-only, on the remote host
       ok  the host says at startup that it accepts unsigned artifacts
       credentials published: 256
       ok  the listener is reachable on loopback


    [the other 102 checks elided: quoted in full in the run above]

    == summary
    all 130 M4 acceptance checks passed against chaos-verify:local

Exit status 0. 102 + 27 + 1 = 130, so this file now describes the lab as it is.

What the 130 lines say about the older transcripts. Compared against the 130-check run in
`remote-provenance-linux-2026-10-02.log`, the `ok` lines here differ in one place, and it is a
measured number rather than a behaviour: a mute peer was given up on after 31s where that run
recorded 30s, in a check whose text is the timeout it observed. Compared against the 102-check
run above, the differences are the 28 added checks plus three such timing figures (`8 -> 7`
and `7 -> 6` in a credential-count check, 4s versus 3s in a connect-wait check, and that same
30s versus 31s). No check text differs in any other way, which is the claim this transcript is
here to keep.

The lab is named on an executable line of `.github/workflows/docker-labs.yml` as of this
change, so the count in the summary line and the count in the script are now kept together by
a schedule rather than by memory.
